 but at the same  time it is kind of you know are not  pudding but just the fact that he's up  in the attic and and especially coming  off the what happened before getting cuz  when when they were at that cliff edge  and she was telling him to jump and he  said he would and then she said no you  should be scared and then he was very  convincingly like that was a that was a  point where like he started being said  I'm like that's really artificial but  then he really started to get a grip on  it as he saw its impact on her and I was  like oh god it went to black and I  thought that was it I thought it was  gonna end like not being clear if she  let him jump if he jumped if she jumped  with him I thought it was just done  there so coming off of that moment to  then the revelation that he didn't jump  but he's just he's in the Attic the same  as the pictures that this guy's mother  had put up there of her other son of her  husband when they passed away because  you loved them too much to get rid of  the memory but you also can't look at it  anymore and that it's just that push and  pull of not wanting to let go but not  being able to deal with the pain of  trying to hold on and that that very  human nature push-pull conflict of what  you want and what does and doesn't work  and now solidified in a walking talking  entity that's in her attic it's it's  just so melancholy just so sad like I  said this one you know doesn't go for  the I mean there are moments that are  a little unnerving but it doesn't go for  the disturbing it doesn't go for the  unsettling it doesn't go for that  sinking sense of dread that I sometimes  get from black mare it just goes for sad  and it it uses this imaginary technology  to really explore how hard it is to let  go how hard it is to move on it's an  exploration of a human response to  something horrible that sometimes  happens to people and it's done really  well it could have been really gimmicky  it could have been really you know  cheesy and really overly blunt or overly  pointed because like I said it's it's an  exploration of grief it doesn't seem to  have a definitive statement on it but it  is a wonderful capsulation and an  exploration of a slightly exaggerated  but at the same time very realistic  reality of what grief feels like and you  know someone's gone and you want them to  still be there but they're not and you  hold on to whatever it is that you've  got around you that reminds you of them  but then it's not actually them and you  you can't bring yourself to get rid of  it but you can't look at it anymore that  it's just it's it's a wonderful  exploration and analogy and embodiment  and literalization of what so many  people have to go through it's really  well done and it feels kind of low-key  compared to some other black mirror  episodes but this is good stuff as this  show has always been for everything that  I've seen up to this point which again  all of season four of season one now  starting season two so I've got this one  to go through then I've got season three  and then I'm done and that'll be it'll  be a little sad but for right now be  right back did you see it what were your  thoughts on it  whatever they were drop something down  the comments let's talk about it there's  all those things you can do things like  support me on patreon or follow me on  Twitter or listen to my podcast  links for all that stuff is down in the  description so until next time this  council is adjourned  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

